# Maintain a project with ResearchHarness

## Purpose

Locate project maintenance procedures, local data and Python bindings, and structure reviews.

## Scope

Use this guide for projects connected to ResearchHarness. The project's README, dependencies, configuration, and commands also provide an independent installation and runtime workflow.

- Read the managed section of project `AGENTS.md` for the ResearchHarness root and project ID. Use `rh projects --harness-root <harness-root> status <project-id>` to inspect the connection. Use `apply` when synchronizing it, and `set --skills on` or `set --skills off` to change the shared skills setting.
- Use `rh projects resources <project-id>` to see local resources and `rh projects bind <project-id>` to update them. Project startup and dependency installation use the same Python binding.
- Use `rh projects structure list <project-id>` and `show` to locate previously recorded structure reviews. Follow the project's review process and the current authorization for changes.
- For onboarding, overall review, or project-wide maintenance, start with `maintain-project`. Check a representative default workflow during onboarding. During project-wide work, reuse evidence and check individual responsibilities where needed.
- Before writing files or launching a command that generates files, classify the operation using the managed section of project `AGENTS.md` and follow its linked procedure. Use the specialist skill for implementation details. Confirm actual destinations after the work and reuse authorized checks.
- Put one-time work under the existing project temporary location. Use the project dependency declarations and bound interpreter for installation. Keep reusable data preparation caches with the corresponding data root.
